# The audit of 32-bit unsigned offsets added to an address on a 64-bit host, as clang-query matchers. On the DS a
# pointer is 32 bits, so a u32 added to it wraps: an offset of 0xffffff00 moves it 256 bytes back. On a host the
# pointer is 64 bits and the u32 is zero-extended, so the same offset moves it 4 GiB forward, and a pointer subscripted
# by such a u32 reaches as far. There is no cast, so the pointer cast guard (check_ptrcasts.py) can't see it. The field
# script read its jump, call and movement offsets so until PR 29 (src/ov012/scrcmd_vm.c, src/ov036/scrcmd_fldmmdl.c
# and src/ov012/script_sys.c, the row of port/docs/game-changes.md), and Black 2's starter scene crashed at its first
# offset back.
#
# An offset is safe as a u32 when it can't be negative: one that counts from the start of its file or of a block, an
# index, a size. One that counts from the reader's own place, as a script's jumps do, can point back, and is read as an
# s32 (an sptr where it is added to an address held in an integer). The matchers list every such sum and subscript in
# the files the command below names, the code that reads offsets from data which the audit has read so far:
# - the script interpreters, the files that include system/vm.h (the field script's commands and system, the trainers'
#   AI, the battle's effect scripts, the musical's scripts);
# - NitroSystem's resource readers, lib/nnsys/src and port/hal/nnsys (the G2D loaders that build the host's structs,
#   which the guard doesn't compile);
# - SPL, lib/spl/src, whose manager reads a particle resource's offsets;
# - the files of NitroSDK's sound, graphics and file system that the host compiles (it replaces the others), the 2D
#   loaders and the BG addresses among them;
# - GFL's archive and message readers (src/gfl/arc_tool.c, arc_util.c and msgdata.c), and the zone's event data
#   (src/ov012/eventdata_system.c), which hands the field script its scene-change table.
# Other code that reads offsets from data, such as the field's map loaders and the apps' own file readers, isn't in the
# command yet: add a reader's files to it when one is suspected, and read each site they bring. port/docs/STATUS.md's
# entry for PR 29's follow-up has the counts and the reading of each site; run it again after a change to one of those
# files or a new interpreter or reader, and read each new site.
#
# Run it from the repository's root on a host build's compile database (Homebrew LLVM's clang-query), and again with
# -p build/port-w2:
#
#   clang-query -p build/port -f port/tools/pointer_offsets.query \
#       $(git grep -l -e '"system/vm.h"' -- src) \
#       $(git ls-files 'lib/nnsys/src/*.c' 'port/hal/nnsys/*.c' 'lib/spl/src/*.c') \
#       lib/nitro/src/snd/{nitro_snd_main,snd_alarm,snd_util,snd_work}.c \
#       lib/nitro/src/gx/{g2,g3_util,g3imm,g3x,gx,gx_bgcnt,gx_load2d,gx_state,gx_vramcnt}.c \
#       lib/nitro/src/fs/{fs_api,fs_archive,fs_file,fs_overlay_digest,fs_rom}.c \
#       src/gfl/{arc_tool,arc_util,msgdata}.c src/ov012/eventdata_system.c
#
# Each match prints its site and then its count ("N matches."), in the order of the matchers below. A site in a header
# shows once for each file that includes it.

set output diag
set bind-root false

# A 32-bit unsigned value (u32 is unsigned int on every host), a pointer, and an address held in an integer: uptr is
# unsigned long on macOS and Linux, where the audit runs. sizeof's results, which are unsigned long too, are sizes
let u32Value ignoringParenImpCasts(expr(hasType(qualType(hasCanonicalType(asString("unsigned int"))))))
let pointerValue ignoringParenImpCasts(expr(hasType(pointerType())))
let addressValue ignoringParenImpCasts(expr(hasType(qualType(hasCanonicalType(asString("unsigned long")))),
    unless(unaryExprOrTypeTraitExpr())))

# 1. A pointer plus or minus a u32
match binaryOperator(hasAnyOperatorName("+", "-"), hasEitherOperand(pointerValue),
    hasEitherOperand(u32Value)).bind("pointer")

# 2. A pointer moved by a u32
match binaryOperator(hasAnyOperatorName("+=", "-="), hasLHS(pointerValue), hasRHS(u32Value)).bind("step")

# 3. An address held in an integer, plus, minus or moved by a u32
match binaryOperator(hasAnyOperatorName("+", "-", "+=", "-="), hasEitherOperand(addressValue),
    hasEitherOperand(u32Value)).bind("address")

# 4. A pointer subscripted by a u32. An array's own subscript isn't listed: its base is the array, whose index counts
# from its start
match arraySubscriptExpr(hasBase(pointerValue), hasIndex(u32Value)).bind("subscript")
